deepening leads found by re-running (signs the topic deserves more):
- M5.34 writes the whole directory (M5.4 wrote 18 parcels, a share of 30
  `0x2046` dirs); the tile grew 4.67 MB once and `tilewriter` appends a grown
  slot with 256-byte padding (tilewriter.py:927-935), so a second map update of
  the same directory grows the tile again over a superseded copy. No size
  budget, no "same input twice -> same bytes" test -> M5.45 (idempotence),
  M7.1 link.
- the highest card rung that exists still splices level-1 only (M5.4/M5.34);
  tilewriter has --level 1|2 and B48R058R does own 4 level-2 dirs (all
  `0x2046`) -> M5.44. While writing it I found the real blocker:
  `clarion_bg.parcel_geo(2,76,940,31,31)` walks to 19.875,53.25 (0.125 deg
  per parcel, 32x32 = 4x2.667 deg) while `parcel_census.directory_bbox` prints
  16..17,50.667..51.333 for the same directory, and the selftest only asserts
  the level-1 span. The two checked-in functions disagree on what a level-2
  directory is by 4x; a wrong grid writes geometry into the wrong place and
  every L2 check still passes (it compares the writer to itself). That is
  M5.44's first half.

